  https://github.com/ilrein/shaderdesk/blob/main/README.md, last section,
  "License": "MIT, see [LICENSE](LICENSE)."

  CONTRIBUTING.md: "By contributing, you agree that your work is released
  under the [MIT License](LICENSE)."

Ilia Reingold (ilrein) is the only contributor to that repository.


WHAT WAS CHANGED

- The ShaderDesk wrapper (scene_frame with its Uniforms, Galaxy, Flare and bg
  arguments, and the shared Common.metal) is replaced by mainImage(). The
  display is the whole render target: q = fragCoord / height, one pixel is
  1 / height.
- The scene clock U.target.z is mod(iTime, 86400.0).
- Agent activity (U.motion.z) is a calm constant 0; the per-display seed
  (U.misc.w) is 0, the first display's value; brightness (U.misc.x) is 1.
- ShaderDesk's present() is written out at the end of mainImage (tone map, sRGB
  encode, 1/255 dither, NaN guard) because the output here is display-ready.
- ShaderDesk's post-process bloom ("bloom: 0.18") is not available in a single
  pass and is dropped.
- hash11, hash12 and hash22 are copied in from ShaderDesk's Common.metal.
- JELLIES and TAU are #defines, `inline` is dropped, pow(x, 2.0) is written
  x * x where x can be negative, and locals are initialised.
The scene's maths, constants and timings are otherwise as the author wrote
them.


THIRD-PARTY CODE

hash11, hash12 and hash22 in main.metal are David Hoskins' "Hash without Sine"
(https://www.shadertoy.com/view/4djSRW), taken via ShaderDesk's Common.metal,
which does not credit him. Their licence is the MIT licence below. The notice
is quoted from the header that David A Roberts' WGSL port of the same shader
carries (https://gist.github.com/davidar/5f9677a0ccfbd63d7a8657ad9af3a856),
because the Shadertoy page itself refuses automated access and could not be
read. An older revision of that shader (a different formula, with "+ 19.19")
is labelled Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 in another copy; the
functions here are the newer revision ("+ 33.33").

The 1/255 dither in present() is the interleaved gradient noise formula
published by Jorge Jimenez (SIGGRAPH 2014), as in the original's Common.metal.


